"CSS Custom Properties for Cascading Variables Module Level 2 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-variables-2/)\nciteturn1289view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-variables-2/#guaranteed-invalid\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 829\nL0: # CSS Custom Properties for Cascading Variables Module Level 2\nL1: \nL2: cite0†Editor’s Draft†www.w3.org , 9 October 2026\nL3: \nL4: More details about this document\nL5: This version:\nL6:     cite1†https://drafts.csswg.org/css-variables-2/ L7: Implementation Report:\nL8:     cite2†https://wpt.fyi/results/css/css-variables†wpt.fyi L9: Issue Tracking:\nL10:     cite3†CSSWG Issues Repository†github.com L11: Editor:\nL12:     cite4†Tab Atkins Jr.†xanthir.com (Google)\nL13: Suggest an Edit for this Spec:\nL14:     cite5†GitHub Editor†github.com L15: Test Suite:\nL16:     cite6†https://wpt.fyi/results/css/css-variables/†wpt.fyi L17: cite7†Copyright†www.w3.org © 2026 cite8†World Wide Web Consortium†www.w3.org . W3C (World Wide Web Consortium)^{®} cite9†liability†www.w3.org , cite10†trademark†www.w3.org and cite11†permissive document license†www.w3.org rules apply.\nL18: \nL19: * * *\nL20: ## Abstract\nL21: \nL22: This module introduces cascading variables as a new primitive value type that is accepted by all CSS properties, and custom properties for defining them.\nL23: \nL24: cite12†CSS†www.w3.org is a language for describing the rendering of structured documents (such as HTML and XML) on screen, on paper, etc.\nL25: ## Status of this document\nL26: \nL27: This is a public copy of the editors’ draft. It is provided for discussion only and may change at any moment. Its publication here does not imply endorsement of its contents by W3C. Don’t cite this document other than as work in progress.\nL28: Please send feedback by cite13†filing issues in GitHub†github.com (preferred), including the spec code “css-variables” in the title, like this: “[css-variables] …summary of comment…”. All issues and comments are cite14†archived†lists.w3.org . Alternately, feedback can be sent to the (cite15†archived†lists.w3.org ) public mailing list www-style@w3.org.\nL29: \nL30: This document is governed by the cite16†18 August 2025 W3C Process Document†www.w3.org .\nL31: ## Table of Contents\nL32:   1. cite17†1 Introduction L33:     1. cite18†1.1 Value Definitions L34:   2. cite19†2 Defining Custom Properties: the --* family of properties L35:     1. cite20†2.1 Custom Property Value Syntax L36:     2. cite21†2.2 Guaranteed-Invalid Values L37:   3. cite22†3 Using Cascading Variables: the var() notation L38:     1. cite23†3.1 Variable Units L39:   4. cite24†4 APIs L40:     1. cite25†4.1 Serializing Custom Properties L41:   5. cite26†5 Changes L42:     1. cite27†5.1 Changes Since the 16 June 2022 CR Snapshot L43:     2. cite28†5.2 Changes Since the 11 November 2021 CR Draft L44:     3. cite29†5.3 Changes Since the 03 December 2015 CR L45:     4. cite30†5.4 Changes since the May 6 2014 Last Call Working Draft L46:   6. cite31†6 Acknowledgments L47:   7. cite32†7 Privacy Considerations L48:   8. cite33†8 Security Considerations L49:   9. cite34†Conformance L50:     1. cite35†Document conventions L51:     2. cite36†Conformance classes L52:     3. cite37†Partial implementations L53:       1. cite38†Implementations of Unstable and Proprietary Features L54:     4. cite39†Non-experimental implementations L55:   10. cite40†Index L56:     1. cite41†Terms defined by this specification L57:     2. cite42†Terms defined by reference L58:   11. cite43†References L59:     1. cite44†Normative References L60:     2. cite45†Non-Normative References L61:   12. cite46†Property Index L62: ## 1. Introduction\nL63: \nL64: This section is not normative.\nL65: \nL66: Large documents or applications (and even small ones) can contain quite a bit of CSS. Many of the values in the CSS file will be duplicate data; for example, a site may establish a color scheme and reuse three or four colors throughout the site. Altering this data can be difficult and error-prone, since it’s scattered throughout the CSS file (and possibly across multiple files), and may not be amenable to Find-and-Replace.\nL67: This module introduces a family of custom author-defined properties known collectively as cite47†custom properties , which allow an author to assign arbitrary values to a property with an author-chosen name, and the cite48†var() function, which allow an author to then use those values in other properties elsewhere in the document.\nL68: This makes it easier to read large files, as seemingly-arbitrary values now have informative names, and makes editing such files much easier and less error-prone, as one only has to change the value once, in the custom property, and the change will propagate to all uses of that variable automatically.\nL69: ### 1.1. Value Definitions\nL70: \nL71: This specification follows the cite49†CSS property definition conventions†www.w3.org from cite50†[CSS2] using the cite51†value definition syntax†www.w3.org from cite52†[CSS-VALUES-3] . Value types not defined in this specification are defined in CSS Values & Units cite52†[CSS-VALUES-3] . Combination with other CSS modules may expand the definitions of these value types.\nL72: In addition to the property-specific values listed in their definitions, all properties defined in this specification also accept the cite53†CSS-wide keywords as their property value. For readability they have not been repeated explicitly.\nL73: ## 2. Defining Custom Properties: the --* family of properties\nL74: \nL75: This specification defines an open-ended set of properties called cite47†custom properties , which, among other things, are used to define the cite54†substitution value of cite48†var() functions.\nL76: Name:  | --*\nL77: cite55†Value:†www.w3.org | cite56†<declaration-value> cite57†? L78: cite58†Initial:†www.w3.org | the cite59†guaranteed-invalid value L79: cite60†Applies to:†www.w3.org | all elements and all pseudo-elements (including those with restricted property lists)\nL80: cite61†Inherited:†www.w3.org | yes\nL81: cite62†Percentages:†www.w3.org | n/a\nL82: cite63†Computed value:†www.w3.org | specified value with variables substituted, or the cite59†guaranteed-invalid value L83: cite64†Canonical order:†www.w3.org | per grammar\nL84: cite65†Animation type:†www.w3.org | discrete\nL85: User agents are expected to support this property on all media, including non-visual ones.\nL86: A custom property is any property whose name starts with two dashes (U+002D HYPHEN-MINUS), like --foo. The <custom-property-name> production corresponds to this: it’s defined as any cite66†<dashed-ident> (a valid cite67†identifier that starts with two dashes), except -- itself, which is reserved for future use by CSS. cite47†Custom properties are solely for use by authors and users; CSS will never give them a meaning beyond what is presented here.\nL87: \nL88: Tests\nL89:   * cite68†variable-declaration-29.html†wpt.fyi cite69†(live test)†wpt.live cite70†(source)†github.com L90:   * cite71†variable-declaration-31.html†wpt.fyi cite72†(live test)†wpt.live cite73†(source)†github.com L91:   * cite74†variable-declaration-32.html†wpt.fyi cite75†(live test)†wpt.live cite76†(source)†github.com L92:   * cite77†variable-declaration-33.html†wpt.fyi cite78†(live test)†wpt.live cite79†(source)†github.com L93:   * cite80†variable-declaration-34.html†wpt.fyi cite81†(live test)†wpt.live cite82†(source)†github.com L94:   * cite83†variable-declaration-35.html†wpt.fyi cite84†(live test)†wpt.live cite85†(source)†github.com L95:   * cite86†variable-declaration-36.html†wpt.fyi cite87†(live test)†wpt.live cite88†(source)†github.com L96:   * cite89†variable-declaration-40.html†wpt.fyi cite90†(live test)†wpt.live cite91†(source)†github.com L97:   * cite92†variable-declaration-41.html†wpt.fyi cite93†(live test)†wpt.live cite94†(source)†github.com L98:   * cite95†variable-declaration-42.html†wpt.fyi cite96†(live test)†wpt.live cite97†(source)†github.com L99:   * cite98†variable-empty-name-reserved.html†wpt.fyi cite99†(live test)†wpt.live cite100†(source)†github.com L100: Custom properties define variables, referenced with the cite48†var() notation, which can be used for many purposes. For example, a page that consistently uses a small set of colors in its design can store the colors in custom properties and use them with variables:\nL101: \nL102:     :root {\nL103:       --main-color: #06c;\nL104:       --accent-color: #006;\nL105:     }\nL106:     /* The rest of the CSS file */\nL107:     #foo h1 {\nL108:       color: var(--main-color);\nL109:     }\nL110: The naming provides a mnemonic for the colors, prevents difficult-to-spot typos in the color codes, and if the theme colors are ever changed, focuses the change on one simple spot (the custom property value) rather than requiring many edits across all stylesheets in the webpage.\nL111: Unlike other CSS properties, custom property names are not cite101†ASCII case-insensitive†infra.spec.whatwg.org . Instead, custom property names are only equal to each other if they are cite102†identical to†infra.spec.whatwg.org each other.\nL112: \nL113: Tests\nL114: \nL115:   * cite103†css-vars-custom-property-case-sensitive-001.html†wpt.fyi cite104†(live test)†wpt.live cite105†(source)†github.com L116: While both --foo and --FOO are valid, they are distinct properties —​using var(--foo) will refer to the first one, while using var(--FOO) will refer to the second.\nL117: Perhaps more surprisingly, --foó and --foó are distinct properties. The first is spelled with U+00F3 (LATIN SMALL LETTER O WITH ACUTE) while the second is spelled with an ASCII \"o\" followed by U+0301 (COMBINING ACUTE ACCENT), and the \"cite102†identical to†infra.spec.whatwg.org \" relation uses direct codepoint-by-codepoint comparison to determine if two strings are equal, to avoid the complexities and pitfalls of unicode normalization and locale-specific collation.\nL118: Operating systems, keyboards, or input methods sometimes encode visually-identical text using different codepoint sequences. Authors are advised to choose variable names that avoid potential confusion or to use escapes and other means to ensure that similar appearing sequences are identical. See Section 2.3 in cite106†[CHARMOD-NORM] for examples.\nL119: Developers maintaining the following CSS might be confused why the test patch is red:\nL120: \nL121:     --fijord: red;\nL122:     --fĳord: green;\nL123:     --ﬁjord: blue;\nL124: \nL125:     .test {\nL126:       background-color: var(--fijord);\nL127:     }\nL128: The reason is that the first custom property uses the character sequence LATIN SMALL LETTER F + LATIN SMALL LETTER I + LATIN SMALL LETTER J; the second, identical-looking one uses the character sequence LATIN SMALL LETTER F + LATIN SMALL LIGATURE IJ while the third uses the character sequence LATIN SMALL LIGATURE FI + LATIN SMALL LETTER J.\nL129: \nL130: So the CSS contains three distinct custom properties, two of which are unused.\nL131: Custom properties are not reset by the cite107†all property. We may define a property in the future that resets all variables.\nL132: \nL133: The cite53†CSS-wide keywords can be used in custom properties, with the same meaning as in any another property.\nL134: \nL135: Tests\nL136:   * cite108†variable-declaration-43.html†wpt.fyi cite109†(live test)†wpt.live cite110†(source)†github.com L137:   * cite111†variable-declaration-44.html†wpt.fyi cite112†(live test)†wpt.live cite113†(source)†github.com L138:   * cite114†variable-declaration-45.html†wpt.fyi cite115†(live test)†wpt.live cite116†(source)†github.com L139:   * cite117†variable-declaration-46.html†wpt.fyi cite118†(live test)†wpt.live cite119†(source)†github.com L140:   * cite120†variable-declaration-47.html†wpt.fyi cite121†(live test)†wpt.live cite122†(source)†github.com L141:   * cite123†variable-declaration-56.html†wpt.fyi cite124†(live test)†wpt.live cite125†(source)†github.com L142:   * cite126†variable-declaration-57.html†wpt.fyi cite127†(live test)†wpt.live cite128†(source)†github.com L143:   * cite129†variable-declaration-58.html†wpt.fyi cite130†(live test)†wpt.live cite131†(source)†github.com L144:   * cite132†variable-declaration-60.html†wpt.fyi cite133†(live test)†wpt.live cite134†(source)†github.com L145:   * cite135†variable-definition-keywords.html†wpt.fyi cite136†(live test)†wpt.live cite137†(source)†github.com L146:   * cite138†variable-css-wide-keywords.html†wpt.fyi cite139†(live test)†wpt.live cite140†(source)†github.com L147: Note: That is, they’re interpreted at cascaded-value time as normal, and are not preserved as the custom property’s value, and thus are not substituted in by the corresponding variable.\nL148: Note: While this module focuses on the use of cite47†custom properties with the cite48†var() function to create “variables”, they can also be used as actual custom properties, parsed by and acted on by script. It’s expected that the CSS Extensions spec cite141†[CSS-EXTENSIONS] will expand on these use-cases and make them easier to do.\nL149: Custom properties are ordinary properties, so they can be declared on any element, are resolved with the normal inheritance and cascade rules, can be made conditional with cite142†@media and other conditional rules, can be used in HTML’s `style` attribute, can be read or set using the CSSOM, etc.\nL150: \nL151: Tests\nL152:   * cite143†css-vars-custom-property-inheritance.html†wpt.fyi cite144†(live test)†wpt.live cite145†(source)†github.com L153:   * cite146†variable-created-document.html†wpt.fyi cite147†(live test)†wpt.live cite148†(source)†github.com L154:   * cite149†variable-created-element.html†wpt.fyi cite150†(live test)†wpt.live cite151†(source)†github.com L155:   * cite152†variable-cssText.html†wpt.fyi cite153†(live test)†wpt.live cite154†(source)†github.com L156:   * cite155†variable-declaration-06.html†wpt.fyi cite156†(live test)†wpt.live cite157†(source)†github.com L157:   * cite158†variable-definition-cascading.html†wpt.fyi cite159†(live test)†wpt.live cite160†(source)†github.com L158:   * cite161†variable-external-declaration-01.html†wpt.fyi cite162†(live test)†wpt.live cite163†(source)†github.com L159:   * cite164†variable-external-reference-01.html†wpt.fyi cite165†(live test)†wpt.live cite166†(source)†github.com L160:   * cite167†variable-external-supports-01.html†wpt.fyi cite168†(live test)†wpt.live cite169†(source)†github.com L161:   * cite170†variable-first-letter.html†wpt.fyi cite171†(live test)†wpt.live cite172†(source)†github.com L162:   * cite173†variable-first-line.html†wpt.fyi cite174†(live test)†wpt.live cite175†(source)†github.com L163:   * cite176†variable-pseudo-element.html†wpt.fyi cite177†(live test)†wpt.live cite178†(source)†github.com L164:   * cite179†variable-reference-13.html†wpt.fyi cite180†(live test)†wpt.live cite181†(source)†github.com L165:   * cite182†variable-reference-14.html†wpt.fyi cite183†(live test)†wpt.live cite184†(source)†github.com L166:   * cite185†variable-reference-shorthands.html†wpt.fyi cite186†(live test)†wpt.live cite187†(source)†github.com L167:   * cite188†variable-reference-visited.html†wpt.fyi cite189†(live test)†wpt.live cite190†(source)†github.com L168: Notably, they can even be animated, but since the UA has no way to interpret their contents, they always use the \"flips at 50%\" behavior that is used for any other pair of values that can’t be intelligently interpolated. However, any cite47†custom property used in a cite191†@keyframes rule becomes animation-tainted, which affects how it is treated when referred to via the cite48†var() function in an animation property.\nL169: \nL170: Tests\nL171:   * cite192†variable-animation-from-to.html†wpt.fyi cite193†(live test)†wpt.live cite194†(source)†github.com L172:   * cite195†variable-animation-over-transition.html†wpt.fyi cite196†(live test)†wpt.live cite197†(source)†github.com L173:   * cite198†variable-animation-substitute-into-keyframe-shorthand.html†wpt.fyi cite199†(live test)†wpt.live cite200†(source)†github.com L174:   * cite201†variable-animation-substitute-into-keyframe-transform.html†wpt.fyi cite202†(live test)†wpt.live cite203†(source)†github.com L175:   * cite204†variable-animation-substitute-into-keyframe.html†wpt.fyi cite205†(live test)†wpt.live cite206†(source)†github.com L176:   * cite207†variable-animation-substitute-within-keyframe-fallback.html†wpt.fyi cite208†(live test)†wpt.live cite209†(source)†github.com L177:   * cite210†variable-animation-substitute-within-keyframe-multiple.html†wpt.fyi cite211†(live test)†wpt.live cite212†(source)†github.com L178:   * cite213†variable-animation-substitute-within-keyframe.html†wpt.fyi cite214†(live test)†wpt.live cite215†(source)†github.com L179:   * cite216†variable-animation-to-only.html†wpt.fyi cite217†(live test)†wpt.live cite218†(source)†github.com L180: Note: Like any other property that animates discretely, custom properties can’t be transitioned. cite219†Registered custom properties†drafts.css-houdini.org can, however, if given a syntax that has non-discrete animation behavior.\nL181: \nL182: cite220†Animation-tainted is \"infectious\": custom properties which reference animation-tainted properties also become animation-tainted.\nL183: \nL184: This style rule:\nL185: \nL186:     :root {\nL187:       --header-color: #06c;\nL188:     }\nL189: declares a cite47†custom property named --header-color on the root element, and assigns to it the value \"#06c\". This property is then inherited to the elements in the rest of the document. Its value can be referenced with the cite48†var() function:\nL190: \nL191:     h1 { background-color: var(--header-color); }\nL192: The preceding rule is equivalent to writing cite221†background-color: #06c; , except that the variable name makes the origin of the color clearer, and if var(--header-color) is used on other elements in the document, all of the uses can be updated at once by changing the --header-color property on the root element.\nL193: If a cite47†custom property is declared multiple times, the standard cascade rules help resolve it. Variables always draw from the computed value of the associated custom property on the same element:\nL194: \nL195:     :root { --color: blue; }\nL196:     div { --color: green; }\nL197:     #alert { --color: red; }\nL198:     * { color: var(--color); }\nL199: \nL200:     <p>I inherited blue from the root element!</p>\nL201:     <div>I got green set directly on me!</div>\nL202:     <div id='alert'>\nL203:       While I got red set directly on me!\nL204:       <p>I'm red too, because of inheritance!</p>\nL205:     </div>\nL206: A real-world example of cite47†custom property usage is easily separating out strings from where they’re used, to aid in maintenance of internationalization:\nL207: \nL208:     :root,\nL209:     :root:lang(en) {--external-link: \"external link\";}\nL210:     :root:lang(el) {--external-link: \"εξωτερικός σύνδεσμος\";}\nL211: \nL212:     a[href^=\"http\"]::after {content: \" (\" var(--external-link) \")\"}\nL213: \nL214: \nL215: The variable declarations can even be kept in a separate file, to make maintaining the translations simpler.\nL216: ### 2.1. Custom Property Value Syntax\nL217: \nL218: The allowed syntax for cite47†custom properties is extremely permissive. The cite56†<declaration-value> production matches any sequence of one or more tokens, so long as the sequence does not contain cite222†<bad-string-token> , cite223†<bad-url-token> , unmatched cite224†<)-token> , cite225†<]-token> , or cite226†<}-token> , or top-level cite227†<semicolon-token> tokens or cite228†<delim-token> tokens with a value of \"!\".\nL219: \nL220: Tests\nL221:   * cite229†long-variable-reference-crash.html†wpt.fyi cite230†(live test)†wpt.live cite231†(source)†github.com L222:   * cite232†test_variable_legal_values.html†wpt.fyi cite233†(live test)†wpt.live cite234†(source)†github.com L223:   * cite235†variable-declaration-15.html†wpt.fyi cite236†(live test)†wpt.live cite237†(source)†github.com L224:   * cite238†variable-declaration-24.html†wpt.fyi cite239†(live test)†wpt.live cite240†(source)†github.com L225:   * cite241†variable-declaration-25.html†wpt.fyi cite242†(live test)†wpt.live cite243†(source)†github.com L226:   * cite244†variable-declaration-26.html†wpt.fyi cite245†(live test)†wpt.live cite246†(source)†github.com L227:   * cite247†variable-declaration-59.html†wpt.fyi cite248†(live test)†wpt.live cite249†(source)†github.com L228: In addition, if the value of a cite47†custom property contains a cite48†var() reference, the var() reference must be valid according to the specified var() grammar. If not, the custom property is invalid and must be ignored.\nL229: \nL230: Note: This definition, along with the general CSS syntax rules, implies that a custom property value never includes an unmatched quote or bracket, and so cannot have any effect on larger syntax constructs, like the enclosing style rule, when reserialized.\nL231: Note: Custom properties can contain a trailing !important, but this is automatically removed from the property’s value by the CSS parser, and makes the custom property \"important\" in the CSS cascade. In other words, the prohibition on top-level \"!\" characters does not prevent !important from being used, as the !important is removed before syntax checking happens.\nL232: \nL233: Tests\nL234:   * cite250†variable-declaration-20.html†wpt.fyi cite251†(live test)†wpt.live cite252†(source)†github.com L235:   * cite253†variable-declaration-23.html†wpt.fyi cite254†(live test)†wpt.live cite255†(source)†github.com L236: \nL237: For example, the following is a valid custom property:\nL238: \nL239:     --foo: if(x > 5) this.width = 10;\nL240: \nL241: \nL242: While this value is obviously useless as a variable, as it would be invalid in any normal property, it might be read and acted on by JavaScript.\nL243: The values of custom properties, and the values of cite48†var() functions substituted into custom properties, are case-sensitive, and must be preserved in their original author-given casing. (Many CSS values are cite101†ASCII case-insensitive†infra.spec.whatwg.org , which user agents can take advantage of by \"canonicalizing\" them into a single casing, but that isn’t allowed for custom properties.)\nL244: \nL245: Tests\nL246:   * cite256†variable-declaration-38.html†wpt.fyi cite257†(live test)†wpt.live cite258†(source)†github.com L247:   * cite259†variable-declaration-39.html†wpt.fyi cite260†(live test)†wpt.live cite261†(source)†github.com L248: Because custom properties can contain anything, there is no general way to know how to interpret what’s inside of them (until they’re substituted into a known property with cite48†var() ). Rather than have them partially resolve in some cases but not others, they’re left completely unresolved; they’re a bare stream of cite262†CSS tokens interspersed with var() functions.\nL249: This has some knock-on implications. For example, relative URLs in CSS are resolved against the base URL of the stylesheet the value appears in. However, if a custom property like --my-image: url(foo.jpg); shows up in an `\"/a/style.css\"` stylesheet, it will not resolve into an absolute URL immediately; if that variable is later used in a different `\"/b/style.css\"` stylesheet like cite263†background: var(--my-image); , it will resolve at that point to `\"/b/foo.jpg\"`.\nL250: ### 2.2. Guaranteed-Invalid Values\nL251: \nL252: The initial value of a cite47†custom property is a guaranteed-invalid value.\nL253: \nL254: The cite59†guaranteed-invalid value is, well, guaranteed to be invalid. If it ever appears in a property value, then at cite264†computed value time that property becomes cite265†invalid at computed-value time .\nL255: \nL256: Non-property contexts will define their own behavior for the cite59†guaranteed-invalid value , but it will always be \"invalid\" in some sense.\nL257: The cite59†guaranteed-invalid value serializes as the empty string, but actually writing an empty value into a custom property, like --foo:;, is a valid (empty) value, not the guaranteed-invalid value. If, for whatever reason, one wants to manually reset a cite47†custom property to the guaranteed-invalid value, using the keyword cite266†initial will do this.\nL258: Note: Other than invoking the cite267†initial value of a non-registered cite47†custom property , the only way to create the cite59†guaranteed-invalid value is by having an invalid cite268†arbitrary substitution function .\nL259: ## 3. Using Cascading Variables: the cite48†var() notation\nL260: \nL261: The value of a cite47†custom property can be substituted into the value of another property with the cite48†var() function. The syntax of var() is:\nL262: \nL263:     var() = var( cite269†<custom-property-name> cite270†, cite56†<declaration-value> cite57†? )\nL264: \nL265: \nL266: The cite48†var() function is an cite268†arbitrary substitution function , and its cite271†argument grammar is:\nL267: \nL268:     cite272†<var-args> = var( cite56†<declaration-value> cite270†, cite56†<declaration-value> cite57†? )\nL269: Tests\nL270:   * cite273†variable-reference-07.html†wpt.fyi cite274†(live test)†wpt.live cite275†(source)†github.com L271:   * cite276†variable-reference-08.html†wpt.fyi cite277†(live test)†wpt.live cite278†(source)†github.com L272:   * cite279†variable-reference-09.html†wpt.fyi cite280†(live test)†wpt.live cite281†(source)†github.com L273:   * cite282†variable-reference-10.html†wpt.fyi cite283†(live test)†wpt.live cite284†(source)†github.com L274:   * cite285†variable-reference-17.html†wpt.fyi cite286†(live test)†wpt.live cite287†(source)†github.com L275:   * cite288†variable-reference-20.html†wpt.fyi cite289†(live test)†wpt.live cite290†(source)†github.com L276:   * cite291†variable-reference-21.html†wpt.fyi cite292†(live test)†wpt.live cite293†(source)†github.com L277:   * cite294†variable-reference-22.html†wpt.fyi cite295†(live test)†wpt.live cite296†(source)†github.com L278:   * cite297†variable-reference-23.html†wpt.fyi cite298†(live test)†wpt.live cite299†(source)†github.com L279:   * cite300†variable-reference-24.html†wpt.fyi cite301†(live test)†wpt.live cite302†(source)†github.com L280:   * cite303†variable-reference-25.html†wpt.fyi cite304†(live test)†wpt.live cite305†(source)†github.com L281:   * cite306†variable-reference-28.html†wpt.fyi cite307†(live test)†wpt.live cite308†(source)†github.com L282:   * cite309†variable-reference-29.html†wpt.fyi cite310†(live test)†wpt.live cite311†(source)†github.com L283:   * cite312†variable-reference-31.html†wpt.fyi cite313†(live test)†wpt.live cite314†(source)†github.com L284:   * cite315†variable-reference-32.html†wpt.fyi cite316†(live test)†wpt.live cite317†(source)†github.com --------------------------------------------